### 3. Discovering Encrypted Client Hello Deployments by Active ECH Probing
- **Presenter:** Matthias Wählisch
- **Slides:** [Discovering Encrypted Client Hello Deployments by Active ECH Probing](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-dinrg-discovering-encrypted-client-hello-deployments-by-active-ech-probing-00)
- **Technical Content:**
  - Presented findings from an IMC paper on active ECH probing across 37 million domains without relying on DNS ECH service records (`HTTPS` records).
  - Leveraged the ECH retry/GREASE mechanism (sending random ECH configurations and detecting server retry responses).
  - Found over 5 million ECH-capable domains; 32% do not advertise ECH via DNS. Meta deploys ECH out-of-band for mobile applications without DNS configuration.
  - Key rotation for ECH keys is rare across operators; ECH connection latency overhead is negligible.
  - Cloudflare validates and filters outer ECH public SNI names, whereas other implementations accept arbitrary outer SNIs.
  - Results and measurement tools are publicly available at `ech.understandingquic.net`.
- **Discussion:**
  - **Andrew Campling:** Pointed out proposals to dynamically randomize outer public ECH names, which could complicate future active probing.
  - **Geoff Huston:** Noted that ECH originally assumed edge-to-edge DNSSEC validation for authenticating ECH keys. Because DNSSEC validation fails to reach the true edge/client, trust was exported to major CDNs.
  - **Lixia Zhang & Matthias Wählisch:** Discussed how security and privacy protocols often inadvertently drive consolidation toward centralized service providers.

